\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Environment constants scale a sector and spare the edge}
\date{2026-11-21}

\p{Fix a \vocabk{sector labeling}{fdip-0121}. After the
elimination in \ref{fdip-0122}, the site factor at each vertex
is constant on each orbit. On the configurations of the
labeling, the site product of the
\vocabk{extended orbit weight}{fdip-0121} equals one constant
#{c} in the coefficient ring. The face and edge factors do not
use the environment weights. The sum of the weight over the sector
equals #{c} times the sum of the face and edge product over the
same sector. A second choice of environment data gives a second
constant #{c'}. The \vocabk{one-edge operator}{fdip-012C} is
built from the double-coset kernel and the class-function
kernel, so the two choices give it the same matrix.}

\proof{
\p{\ref{fdip-0122} replaces each site factor by
#{c_x=\alpha_{0,x}+\alpha_{1,x}} and proves that #{c_x} is
constant on every orbit. For a configuration of the labeling,
the value #{\phi_x} lies in the orbit #{\mathcal O_{\lambda(x)}},
so #{c_x(\phi_x)} equals the value of #{c_x} at the reference
point of that orbit. The site product is the product of these
finitely many constants. Call the product #{c}. The face product
and the edge product in the weight do not use the environment
functions. Factoring #{c} out of the finite sum gives the
stated multiple. The second choice is the same argument with
the second pair of bit weights. The formula for the one-edge
operator in the definition of the
[one-edge operator](fdip-012C) names only the two kernels on
#{G}. Neither kernel reads the bit weights, so the matrix is
the same for both choices.}
}
